<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heng Samrin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heng Samrin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s Eastern Zone who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1978 to escape the purges. After the Vietnamese invasion of January 1979 he became head of state of the People's Republic of Kampuchea and leader of its ruling party, though real influence passed increasingly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>. He remained a senior figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Cambodian People's Party</a> and later served for many years as president of the National Assembly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heng-samrin.jpg" alt="Heng Samrin" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hul Reaksmey/VOA Khmer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heng_Samrin_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After repeated attacks by the Khmer Rouge regime of Democratic Kampuchea on border villages, Vietnamese forces invaded Cambodia on 25 December 1978, took Phnom Penh on 7 January 1979 and installed the People’s Republic of Kampuchea under <b>Heng Samrin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Vietnam invaded Democratic Kampuchea, its forces took Phnom Penh on 7 January 1979, and the People's Republic of Kampuchea was proclaimed, led by the Kampuchean United Front for National Salvation, a group of former Khmer Rouge cadres who had defected to Vietnam, including <b>Heng Samrin</b> and Hun Sen.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-kampuchea">People's Republic of Kampuchea</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling party, renamed the Cambodian People's Party in 1991, governed under <b>Heng Samrin</b> and Prime Minister Hun Sen while negotiating with the resistance factions.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cambodia">State of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
